@startuml flow-google-signin
' Google sign-in, including the two ways it is refused.
'
' Render:  plantuml docs/diagrams/flow-google-signin.puml

' Explicit near-white, NOT transparent: the text is dark, and a transparent background
' renders black on a dark viewer, which makes the whole diagram unreadable.
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #59606F
  LifeLineBorderColor #C3CAD8
  ParticipantBorderColor #5B5BD6
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #FDFDFD
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFF8E1
  BorderColor #C2670A
}

title Google sign-in

actor User
participant Browser
participant "Backend\n(Spring Security)" as Spring
participant OAuth2SuccessHandler as Success
participant OAuthSignInService as SignIn
database users
participant "SPA\n/oauth/callback" as SPA

User -> Browser : clicks "Continue with Google"
Browser -> Spring : GET /oauth2/authorization/google

note right of Spring
  Both hops must be the same origin. Spring keeps
  the pending authorization request in a session
  cookie; start on one origin and return to another
  and the callback dies on a state mismatch. That is
  why production points straight at the backend and
  not through the Vercel rewrite.
end note

Spring --> Browser : 302 to Google
Browser -> Google : consent
Google --> Browser : 302 back with ?code=...
Browser -> Spring : GET /login/oauth2/code/google
Spring -> Spring : exchange the code, load the profile

alt the provider declined, or state mismatched
  Spring -> SPA : redirect #error=... (OAuth2FailureHandler)
else identity verified
  Spring -> Success : onAuthenticationSuccess(principal)
  Success -> SignIn : signIn(email, name, picture)

  alt no email in the profile
    SignIn --> Success : **refused**
    note right of SignIn : Matching is by address.\nWithout one there is nothing to match.
  else an account already uses that address
    SignIn -> users : find by email
    SignIn --> Success : session for the existing account
    note right of SignIn #E8F5E9
      Signs in with the role it **already has**.
      No setRole anywhere on this path — the
      provider proves who somebody is, it does
      not decide what they may do.
    end note
  else unknown address, auto-provisioning OFF
    SignIn --> Success : **refused**
    note right of SignIn #FFE0E0
      The default. Otherwise anyone who reaches
      the sign-in page becomes a user by clicking
      one button.
    end note
  else unknown address, auto-provisioning ON
    SignIn -> users : create with the configured default role,\nno password hash, generated username
    SignIn --> Success : session for the new account
    note right of SignIn
      Startup **fails** if that default role is a
      privileged one, so this can never mint an admin.
      The generated username is one its owner has
      never seen — which is why sign-in must also
      accept an email.
    end note
  end

  alt refused
    Success -> SPA : redirect #error=<message>
    note right of Success #FFE0E0
      Every refusal is caught here. Letting one
      propagate renders the backend's error page,
      on the backend's domain, to somebody who
      clicked a button on the frontend — it reads
      as a crash with no way back.
    end note
  else signed in
    Success -> SPA : redirect #token=<refreshToken>
    note right of Success
      The **fragment**, never the query string: a
      fragment is not sent to any server, so it stays
      out of access logs and Referer headers.
    end note
  end
end

SPA -> SPA : read the fragment, then history.replaceState
SPA -> Spring : POST /auth/refresh
note over SPA
  Spent immediately, so the value that briefly sat
  in the address bar is already rotated away.
end note
SPA --> User : dashboard

@enduml
